A||||7||Johnson's85SE||Blue Intakes.||||1||
Z||000000||Johnson's85SE||12-07-2000||06:38 PM||||Hi, <BR>Quick question. This backyard mechanic installed a new block on my 2.8. I seen the two engines side by side. The Intake manifold on the newer one, was Blue, and said Multi tuned port injection. It looked Identical to the Fiero stock engine. He transplanted everything from the old block to the new. the only differenece was the dip stick in now in the back by the firewall. It has ran great! Anyone know what kind of car this engine is from? I got a awesome deal from this shady charactor, That's why i never questioned him. Thanks,<BR>Chris<P>------------------<BR>If it isn't broke, it's not my car.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||12-08-2000||08:34 AM||||I am not sure what car that engine might be from, but if you were wanting it on your car, you can just paint the manifold with Hi-Temp engine paint. I am going to be doing it on my rebuild. The TB and middle and lower manifold will be white, and the upper manifold and valve covers will be red. Just a question, where was your dipstick before? Mine has always been in the rear by the firewall.||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||12-08-2000||08:41 AM||||Probley eather from a Caviler or Beretta<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<BR>Front - AudioBahn 4 x 10<BR>Headrest - AudioBahn 3 1/2<P> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Johnson's85SE||12-08-2000||02:12 PM||||On my old engine, it was on the right side of the engine. Not between the firewall and the rear of the engine. <BR>Thanks,<BR>Chris<P>------------------<BR>If it isn't broke, it's not my car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-08-2000||10:55 PM||||The few cavaliers I've seen had red intake.<P>You should have questioned it more. Those really good prices can mean stolen parts. I would not want to be you if that's the case. If it's not hot and just a good deal then shut up and be happy.....||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||12-09-2000||05:31 AM||||The difference between the Berreta/Cavelier/Firebird/Camaro etc... 2.8 V6 engines were that the Fiero had larger valves. Had you taken the long block (block and valves) you would be wondering where all your power went.<P>Ive seen the intake manifold you are refering to on the V6 of a Firebird. As long as you kept your valve heads from your Fiero than you are in good shape.<P>Now you get to worry about the condition the bearings were in on the "new" block....||||reg||14
Z||000006||bubbajoexx||12-09-2000||10:08 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Johnson's85SE:<BR><B>Hi, <BR>Quick question. This backyard mechanic installed a new block on my 2.8. I seen the two engines side by side. The Intake manifold on the newer one, was Blue, and said Multi tuned port injection. It looked Identical to the Fiero stock engine. He transplanted everything from the old block to the new. the only differenece was the dip stick in now in the back by the firewall. It has ran great! Anyone know what kind of car this engine is from? I got a awesome deal from this shady charactor, That's why i never questioned him. Thanks,<BR>Chris<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>the block you are talking about is from a 1985 celebrity euro sport(first year of production)  chevs mid size front drive it used the exactly the same mounts as the fiero but the dipstik is on the firewall side of the engine when installed in a fiero. the only 85 celebrity with fuel injection.<p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FieroGTRacingInc||12-09-2000||11:50 AM||||Is the oil dipstick supposed to be on the fiewall or trunk side??  I've had 2 v6 and both were on the firewall side..  steve||||reg||1
